<style>:root{--bg:#fff;--ink:#202124;--muted:#5f6368;--rule:#dadce0;--accent:#174ea6;--paper:#fff}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#f1f3f4;color:var(--ink);font:16px/1.7 Georgia,"Times New Roman",serif}main{max-width:900px;margin:2rem auto;padding:3rem 4.5rem;background:var(--paper);box-shadow:0 2px 14px #0000001f}h1{text-align:center;margin:0 0 1.5rem;font-size:2rem}h2{margin-top:2.4rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);padding-bottom:.35rem}h3{color:var(--accent);margin-top:1.8rem}.box{margin:1rem 0;padding:.8rem 1.2rem;border-left:4px solid var(--accent);background:#f8f9fa}.formula{text-align:center;overflow:auto;margin:1rem 0;font-size:1.05em}li{margin:.35rem 0}@media(max-width:700px){main{margin:0;padding:2rem 1.25rem;box-shadow:none}h1{font-size:1.6rem}}</style>
<script>window.MathJax={tex:{inlineMath:[['$','$'],['\\(','\\)']]},svg:{fontCache:'global'}};</script><script async src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3/es5/tex-svg.js"></script></head>
<body><main><h1>Honours Linear Algebra<br><small>Week 1 Notes</small></h1>
<h2>1. Systems of Linear Equations</h2><p>A linear system has the form \(AX=b\), where \(A\) is the coefficient matrix.</p><p>For the system \(3x+2y+z=39\), \(2x+3y+z=34\), \(x+2y+3z=26\), the augmented matrix reduces as follows:</p><div class="formula">\[\begin{bmatrix}3&2&1&39\\2&3&1&34\\1&2&3&26\end{bmatrix}\longrightarrow\begin{bmatrix}3&2&1&39\\0&5&1&24\\0&0&36&99\end{bmatrix}.\]</div><p>Back substitution gives \(z=11/4\), \(y=17/4\), and \(x=17/4\).</p>
<h2>2. Matrices</h2><p>An \(m\times n\) matrix has \(m\) rows and \(n\) columns. The identity matrix is \(I_n\), and the transpose is \(A^T\). Matrix addition and scalar multiplication are entrywise.</p><p>Upper and lower triangular matrices have zero entries below or above the diagonal. A diagonal matrix has zero off-diagonal entries.</p>
<h2>3. Elementary Row Operations</h2><ol><li>Interchange two rows.</li><li>Multiply a row by a nonzero scalar.</li><li>Add a multiple of one row to another row.</li></ol><div class="box"><b>Why they work.</b> Every row operation is reversible, so the original and transformed systems have exactly the same solutions.</div><p>Gaussian elimination produces row echelon form. Reduced row echelon form additionally has pivot entries equal to 1 and zeroes elsewhere in each pivot column.</p><h3>Free variables</h3><p>From \(x_1+2x_3=3\), \(x_2-x_3=4\), set \(x_3=t\). Then \(X=(3,4,0)^T+t(-2,1,1)^T\). A row \([0\ 0\ 0\mid c]\), \(c\ne0\), means the system is inconsistent.</p>
<h2>4. Inverse Matrices</h2><p>To compute \(A^{-1}\), row reduce \([A\mid I]\) to \([I\mid A^{-1}]\). If the left block cannot become the identity, \(A\) is not invertible.</p><div class="box"><b>Useful identities.</b> If \(A,B\) are invertible, \((AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}\). Also \((A^T)^{-1}=(A^{-1})^T\).</div><p>A triangular matrix with nonzero diagonal entries is invertible, and its inverse has the same triangular shape.</p>
<h2>5. LU Decomposition</h2><p>If elimination reduces \(A\) to an upper triangular matrix \(U\) without row swaps, then \(A=LU\), where \(L\) is lower triangular.</p><div class="formula">\[L=\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\2/3&1&0\\1/3&4/5&1\end{bmatrix},\quad U=\begin{bmatrix}3&2&1\\0&5/3&1/3\\0&0&12/5\end{bmatrix}.\]</div><p>To solve \(Ax=b\), solve \(Ld=b\) by forward substitution, then \(Ux=d\) by back substitution.</p>
<h2>6. Vector Spaces and Null Spaces</h2><p>\(\nul(A)=\{X\in\mathbb R^n:AX=0\}\) is the null space of \(A\).</p><div class="box"><b>Subspace proof.</b> If \(u,v\in\nul(A)\) and \(a\in\mathbb R\), then \(A(au+v)=aAu+Av=0\). Thus the null space is closed under linear combinations and is a subspace.</div><p>The span of a set is the collection of all finite linear combinations of its vectors, and is always a subspace.</p>
<h2>7. Classifying Solutions</h2><p>After row reduction, a system has no solution if a row \([0\ \cdots\ 0\mid c]\), with \(c\ne0\), appears. It has one solution when every variable is a pivot variable, and infinitely many solutions when it is consistent with at least one free variable.</p><h3>Example with two free variables</h3><p>For \(x_1+2x_3-x_4=3\) and \(x_2-x_3+2x_4=1\), set \(x_3=s\), \(x_4=t\). Then</p><div class="formula">\[X=\begin{bmatrix}3\\1\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}+s\begin{bmatrix}-2\\1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}+t\begin{bmatrix}1\\-2\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}.\]</div><p>The solution set is an affine plane in \(\mathbb R^4\).</p><h3>Inconsistency</h3><p>The row \([0\ 0\ 0\mid5]\) means \(0=5\), so no choice of free variables can produce a solution.</p>
<h2>8. Homogeneous Systems</h2><p>A homogeneous system has the form \(AX=0\), and is always consistent because \(X=0\) is a solution. If there are more unknowns than equations, then at least one variable is free, so a nonzero solution exists. The number of free variables measures the independent directions in the null space.</p>
<h2>9. Matrices as Transformations</h2><p>An \(m\times n\) matrix defines \(T_A:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m\), \(T_A(X)=AX\). If \(A=[a_1\ \cdots\ a_n]\), then</p><div class="formula">\[AX=x_1a_1+\cdots+x_na_n.\]</div><p>Thus \(AX=b\) is solvable exactly when \(b\) lies in the span of the columns of \(A\).</p>
<h2>10. Elementary Matrices</h2><p>Every row operation is multiplication on the left by an elementary matrix. Since row operations are reversible, elementary matrices are invertible. If \(E_k\cdots E_1A=U\), the elimination process itself has become a matrix factorization, explaining the connection with inverses and LU decomposition.</p>
<h2>11. Inverses in Practice</h2><p>For a square matrix, compute the inverse by reducing \([A\mid I]\) to \([I\mid A^{-1}]\). For \(A=\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{bmatrix}\),</p><div class="formula">\[A^{-1}=\frac1{ad-bc}\begin{bmatrix}d&-b\\-c&a\end{bmatrix},\qquad ad-bc\ne0.\]</div><p>For example, \(\begin{bmatrix}1&2\\3&7\end{bmatrix}^{-1}=\begin{bmatrix}7&-2\\-3&1\end{bmatrix}\).</p>
<h2>12. LU Decomposition</h2><p>Elimination multipliers are stored below the diagonal. Thus \(A=LU\), with \(L\) lower triangular and \(U\) upper triangular. To solve \(Ax=b\), solve \(Ly=b\) by forward substitution and then \(Ux=y\) by back substitution. The same factorization can be reused for many right-hand sides.</p><div class="box"><b>Practice.</b> For \(L=\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\2&1&0\\-1&3&1\end{bmatrix}\) and \(b=(2,5,1)^T\), forward substitution gives \(y=(2,1,2)^T\).</div>
<h2>13. Null Spaces and Affine Solutions</h2><p>If \(AX=b\) is consistent and \(X_0\) is one solution, then every solution is</p><div class="formula">\[X=X_0+Z,\qquad Z\in\operatorname{Nul}(A).\]</div><p>Indeed, \(AX=b\) is equivalent to \(A(X-X_0)=0\). The null space therefore gives exactly the directions along which a particular solution can move without changing \(AX\).</p>
<h2>14. Span, Column Space, and Checklist</h2><p>For vectors \(v_1,\ldots,v_r\), \(\operatorname{Span}\{v_1,\ldots,v_r\}\) is the set of all linear combinations. It is the smallest subspace containing those vectors. The column space of \(A\) is the span of its columns.</p><ol><li>Write the augmented matrix.</li><li>Use reversible row operations.</li><li>Check for contradiction rows.</li><li>Identify pivots and free variables.</li><li>Parametrize the solution.</li><li>Interpret it using column space or null space.</li></ol>
<h2>Final Summary</h2><p>The central workflow is to represent equations as a matrix, simplify by reversible operations, and interpret pivots and free variables. Inverses, LU decomposition, null spaces, spans, and column spaces are compatible languages for the same linear structure.</p></main></body></html>

